Main code compiles. Now step 8: write tests. New test file `OutageApiTests.java` with a controllable clock.

Clock mocking: use `@MockitoBean Clock clock` (Spring Boot 3.4+; 3.5.16 has it). Then stub `clock.instant()` with an AtomicReference<Instant>. WaterService calls `clock.instant()` only. 

But wait — existing tests (WaterApiTests, TransferApiTests) don't mock Clock; they get the real system clock bean. Different context for OutageApiTests due to @MockitoBean. Good.

Test plan for OutageApiTests:

Setup helpers: createWindow (via HTTP), submitAllocation, approve, transfer etc. Fixed test clock: start at Instant "2026-09-26T00:00:00Z", mutable via AtomicReference.

Tests:

1. `createOutageValidatesWindowAndVersion`:
   - create window on channel (future times, e.g. 2026-10-01).
   - create outage with wrong expectedVersion (5) → 409 CHANNEL_VERSION_CONFLICT.
   - create outage expectedVersion 0 → 200, channelVersion 1.
   - overlapping outage (version 1) → 409 OUTAGE_OVERLAP.
   - adjacent outage → 200, channelVersion 2.
   - different channel same period → 200.
   - invalid: start>=end → 400; missing expectedVersion → 400.
   - same outageKey reuse → 409.
   - affected allocation on another channel → 409; nonexistent allocation → 404.

2. `startedOutageCannotBeDeletedOnlyRecovered`:
   - clock at T0. Window in past relative? Window times: supply window 2026-09-20..2026-09-21 (past relative to clock 2026-09-26). Outage 2026-09-25T00:00 .. 2026-09-27T00:00 → started (now 2026-09-26 within).
   - delete → 409 OUTAGE_ALREADY_STARTED.
   - recover with recoveredUtc before now → 400.
   - recover with recoveredUtc = now+1h → 200, status SCHEDULED, recoveredUtc set.
   - recover again → 409 OUTAGE_ALREADY_RECOVERED.
   - delete after started → still 409.
   - Also: not-started outage delete → 200, then recover deleted → 409 OUTAGE_DELETED, delete again → 409 OUTAGE_DELETED.

3. `settlementBlockedByEffectiveOutage`:
   - window 2026-09-20..2026-09-21 past? Wait — supply window times don't matter for operations; only outage intersection matters. Window 2026-09-20T00:00–2026-09-21T00:00, planned 10. Submit allocation 5, approve. 
   - outage on channel 2026-09-20T12:00–2026-09-22T00:00 (intersects window; started since clock 2026-09-26? No wait — outage end 2026-09-22 < now 2026-09-26 → already ended. Is an ended outage "effective"? My listEffectiveOutages doesn't check end vs now! An outage that ended in the past still intersects the window and has no recovery → blocks settlement forever. Hmm. Is that right? "若申请窗口与任何生效停运窗口相交即422" — "生效停运窗口" = effective outage window. An outage whose period has fully passed... the supply window is also in the past. Settling water for a past window during which the canal was down — should still be blocked (the water couldn't have been delivered). I think keeping it blocked is defensible and simpler: effectiveness = not deleted && not recovered-before-now. Recovery is the way to unblock. OK.
   - So: settle → 422 OUTAGE_INTERSECT. settlement-check → settleable=false, code OUTAGE_INTERSECT.
   - recover outage at now+1h? No — recovery must be < end... end is 2026-09-22 < now → OUTAGE_ALREADY_ENDED 409. Hmm, then this outage can never be lifted. For the test, better: outage 2026-09-20T12:00 – 2026-09-30T00:00 (started, ends in future). Settle → 422. Recover at now+1h → still 422 (recovery in future). Advance clock past recovery → settle succeeds. That tests "恢复仅影响之后的核销" beautifully with the controllable clock.
   - Also risk: allocation approved-unsettled → risk written at outage creation. Transfer of that allocation as source → 409 SUPPLY_RISK. And transfer during effective outage → 422 anyway; check risk gate separately: create risk via outage, then recover outage (recovered=now, advance clock), then transfer → 409 SUPPLY_RISK (risk persists even after recovery — risks are immutable).

4. `settleHappyPathAndBalance`:
   - window planned 10, allocation 5 approved. settle 2 → 200, held 3. settle 4 → 422 INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE. settle 3 → 200, held 0. settle again → 422. Non-approved allocation settle → 409. settlement-check reflects.
   - settlementKey reuse → 409.

5. `batchSettleAllOrNothing`:
   - two allocations approved 5 and 3. Batch [a1=2, a2=1] → 200, held 3 and 2, settlements list size 2.
   - Batch [a1=1, a2=5] → 422 INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE; verify held unchanged (3 and 2) and no settlement rows (settle again same amounts... check via history heldAmount and settlement-check; also batchKey not occupied: re-use same batchKey with valid items → should succeed since failure doesn't occupy... wait batchKey occupancy: existsSettlementBatch checks settlement rows; failed batch wrote none → batchKey reusable. And commandKey: failed command rolled back → commandKey reusable too. Test: retry with same commandKey+batchKey and corrected amounts → 200.
   - Batch with outage-intersecting allocation → 422 OUTAGE_INTERSECT, all rolled back.

6. `transferRespectsOutageAndRisk`:
   - window intersecting effective outage → transfer → 422 OUTAGE_INTERSECT.
   - risk source → 409 SUPPLY_RISK (after recovery as in test 3, or separate: create outage affecting source, delete? can't delete started. Use future outage? Risk written at creation regardless of start time. Create outage in future (not started) affecting approved source → risk written. Transfer now: outage not yet effective? Effectiveness doesn't check start vs now! listEffectiveOutages: intersect && not deleted && (recovered null || recovered > now). A future outage intersecting the window blocks transfers even before it starts. Hmm — is that desired? "若申请窗口与任何生效停运窗口相交即422" — the outage window intersects the supply window; the outage is "生效" (in effect = scheduled, not deleted/recovered). I think blocking is intended: the water can't be delivered during the outage, so settling/transferring quota for that window is blocked. OK so future outage blocks too.
   - So: create future outage affecting source allocation → transfer → 422 OUTAGE_INTERSECT (also risk exists). To isolate SUPPLY_RISK from OUTAGE_INTERSECT: delete the future outage (allowed, not started) → outage no longer effective → transfer → 409 SUPPLY_RISK (risk immutable). 

7. `outageIdempotency`:
   - create outage with commandKey K → 200. Replay same body → 200 same response. Same K different params → 409.
   - failing command (e.g. overlap) with commandKey K2 → 409; then reuse K2 with valid non-overlapping params → 200 （失败不占键）.

8. `concurrentSettlementsNeverOverdraw`:
   - allocation approved 1.000. 10 threads each settle 0.2 with distinct keys → exactly 5 succeed, 5 get 422. held = 0.

9. `concurrentOutageAndSettleCommitOrder`:
   - allocation approved, no outage. Concurrently: create outage (intersecting) vs settle. Exactly one of: settle succeeds (committed before outage) or settle 422 (after outage). If settle succeeded, outage creation still succeeds (outage doesn't check settlements) — hmm, both can succeed: settle commits first, then outage commits. That's fine per commit-order adjudication. So assert: settle either 200 or 422; outage create 200; final state consistent: if settle 200 → held reduced; subsequent settle → 422.
   - Actually with channel row locking, they're serialized: if settle locks channel first, outage waits; settle commits; outage then created. If outage first, settle sees outage → 422. Both orders valid. Assert invariant: after both, further settle → 422; and if first settle returned 200, held reduced by amount.

10. Query tests: impact endpoint returns affected allocations + risks; risks endpoint; settlement-check codes. (covered within above tests, but add explicit not-found cases: outage impact 404, risks 404, settlement-check 404.)

Also channel version increments across delete/recover — assert channelVersion in responses.

Clock control: `@MockitoBean Clock clock` with lenient stubbing. In `@BeforeEach`: `now.set(BASE); lenient().when(clock.instant()).thenAnswer(inv -> now.get());`. Since each test method gets fresh mock? @MockitoBean is reset between tests by default (Mockito reset after each test). Stubbing in @BeforeEach works.

Note: WaterService is constructed once per context with the mock Clock — mock instance persists across tests in same class, reset between tests. Re-stub in @BeforeEach. Good.

Mockito strictness: unnecessary stubbing exceptions — use `lenient()`.

Time base: BASE = 2026-09-26T00:00:00Z. Windows: use times around BASE.

Careful: window creation requires start < end but no relation to now. Approvals etc. don't check time. Good.

One more check — `lockChannel` inside `settle` etc.: `ensureChannel` does findChannel then insert. Window creation already ensures channel exists. OK.

Potential issue: H2 `channel` table name — is CHANNEL a reserved word in H2? H2 reserved keywords: SELECT, etc. CHANNEL is not a reserved word in H2 2.x (it's a non-reserved keyword maybe). In MySQL mode... MySQL 8 doesn't reserve CHANNEL. H2 2.x keywords list: CHANNEL is not in H2's reserved keywords. Should be fine; tests will confirm.

Another concern: `requireKey` for channelId path variable — path variables with special chars fine.

Also `CreateOutageRequest.expectedVersion` is Long — JSON number. Missing → null → 400. Good.

Batch params string could exceed 2048 for large batches — tests use small batches. Fine.

Now about `checkSettlement` GET with query param amount — parseAmount throws 400 for invalid. OK.

One thing in `settleBatch`: `windows.computeIfAbsent(allocation.windowId(), repository::findWindowById)` — computeIfAbsent doesn't allow null values... if findWindowById returns null, computeIfAbsent just doesn't put. Window always exists (FK). OK.

Also in `settleBatch`, `requireSettable` uses locked allocation — good.

Potential deadlock in batch: locks channels sorted, then allocations sorted by id. Transfer locks channel then window then allocations (source then target — not sorted by id!). Existing transfer locks source then target in fixed order (source first). Batch locks allocations sorted by id. Could deadlock: transfer holds alloc A (source, id 5), wants alloc B (target, id 3); batch holds alloc B (id 3 first), wants alloc A (id 5). Both hold channel? Transfer holds channel C, batch holds channel C too — but channel lock is acquired FIRST by both, so only one of them holds the channel at a time; the other waits on channel before touching allocations. Same channel → serialized. Different channels: transfer's window is one channel; batch spanning two channels locks both sorted. Transfer locks channel C1, allocs in window on C1. Batch locks C1 and C2 (sorted) — blocks on C1 until transfer done. No cycle since batch doesn't hold anything while waiting for C1 (it locks channels first, in order, before allocations). Wait — batch locks C1 (held by transfer) → waits, holding C2 (if C2 < C1 sorted first). Transfer needs only C1 → completes → releases. OK no deadlock. Two transfers same window: serialized by channel+window. Two batches overlapping channels: both lock channels in sorted order → no cycle. Good.

